\chapter{Classical and Relativistic Gravitation}
\label{chap:gravitation}

\section{Introduction}
Negative mass astrodynamics requires a precise understanding of the classical
and relativistic gravitational frameworks it extends. This chapter establishes
the baseline equations, tensor structures, and conservation laws that the
Bondi--Hermann formalism modifies. By grounding the simulation engine in
Einsteinian and Newtonian mechanics, we ensure that exotic-matter behaviors
remain mathematically consistent with established physical invariants.

\section{Newtonian Gravitation}
Classical gravity describes the force between two masses $m_1$ and $m_2$ as:
\begin{equation}
    \vb{F}_{12}
    = -\,\frac{G m_1 m_2}{r^2}\,\hat{\vb{r}}_{12},
    \label{eq:newton-grav}
\end{equation}
where $G$ is the gravitational constant, $r$ is the separation distance, and
$\hat{\vb{r}}_{12}$ is the unit vector from particle 1 to particle 2.

Newton's second law provides the inertial response:
\begin{equation}
    \vb{a} = \frac{\vb{F}}{m_i}.
    \label{eq:newton-second}
\end{equation}

In standard physics, $m_i = m_g > 0$. Chapter~\ref{chap:bondi-hermann} removes
this constraint.

\section{General Relativity Foundations}
General Relativity describes gravity not as a force, but as curvature of
spacetime. The metric tensor $g_{\mu\nu}$ defines the geometry:
\begin{equation}
    ds^2 = g_{\mu\nu} dx^\mu dx^\nu.
\end{equation}

The Einstein field equations relate curvature to energy-momentum:
\begin{equation}
    G_{\mu\nu} = 8\pi G\, T_{\mu\nu},
    \label{eq:einstein}
\end{equation}
where $G_{\mu\nu}$ is the Einstein tensor and $T_{\mu\nu}$ is the stress-energy
tensor.

\subsection{Stress-Energy Tensor}
For a perfect fluid:
\begin{equation}
    T_{\mu\nu}
    = (\rho + p) u_\mu u_\nu + p\, g_{\mu\nu},
\end{equation}
where $\rho$ is energy density, $p$ is pressure, and $u_\mu$ is the four-velocity.

Negative mass states require $\rho < 0$, violating classical energy conditions.

\section{Energy Conditions}
The Weak Energy Condition (WEC) requires:
\begin{equation}
    T_{\mu\nu} u^\mu u^\nu \ge 0.
\end{equation}

Negative mass violates:
\begin{equation}
    \rho < 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad T_{\mu\nu} u^\mu u^\nu < 0.
\end{equation}

This violation is essential for:
\begin{itemize}
    \item exotic matter,
    \item wormhole stability,
    \item Alcubierre-class warp metrics,
    \item negative energy density fields.
\end{itemize}

\section{Geodesic Motion}
Particles follow geodesics defined by:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d^2 x^\mu}{d\tau^2}
    + \Gamma^\mu_{\alpha\beta}
      \frac{dx^\alpha}{d\tau}
      \frac{dx^\beta}{d\tau}
    = 0,
\end{equation}
where $\Gamma^\mu_{\alpha\beta}$ are Christoffel symbols.

Negative inertial mass reverses the sign of the geodesic acceleration term,
producing inverted motion trajectories.

\section{Newtonian Limit of General Relativity}
In weak-field, low-velocity regimes:
\begin{equation}
    g_{00} \approx -\left(1 + 2\Phi\right),
\end{equation}
where $\Phi$ is the gravitational potential.

The acceleration becomes:
\begin{equation}
    \vb{a} = -\nabla \Phi.
\end{equation}

Negative gravitational mass flips the sign of $\Phi$, producing repulsive gravity.

